  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>To reach this beach, first park at the Parking des Terrasses, right next to the Port Grimaud tourist office (paid parking from April to October), go through the Port Grimaud Sud barriers, follow the road to the right, then continue straight ahead for about 10 minutes (Grimaud tourist office). It is not wheelchair accessible.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>As on all eight beaches of the town of Grimaud, access is regulated by a municipal bylaw, cited by the Grimaud tourist office (text of the order not consulted): from mid-September to the end of May, dogs are accepted all day without restriction; from 1 June to 15 September, they are welcome from 9pm to 6am, and if they swim, it must be outside the marked bathing zones (15 June to 15 September). A leash is requested on the beaches, and remember waste bags.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: bring water for your dog and something to pick up its waste for the roughly 10-minute walk from the car park: the tourist office lists no shower or toilets on this beach.</div>

      <h2>On the beach</h2>
      <p>The address given by the Grimaud tourist office is rue de la Giscle, plage de Port Grimaud 2, 83310 Grimaud; the beach is a sandbank facing Saint-Tropez, with the lake-city at your back, free access and open every day. From 15 June to 15 September, the bathing zone is marked out, and posidonia is put back into the sea or moved along the beach to clear the shoreline; the sand sifter passes every day between 2am and 9am and waste is collected and removed daily. An emergency call point is nearby, the beach holds the Bathing Water Quality label, and smoking is banned there (decree of 27 June 2025).</p>
      <p>Horses are banned from the beach from 15 May to 15 September. The tourist office reports no lifeguard post or facilities on this beach, unlike Port Grimaud beach, at the end of avenue de la Mer, which has a lifeguard post, showers and public toilets. For any question, the tourist office can be reached on 04 94 55 43 83. It lists Port Grimaud Sud beach among the dog-accessible beaches of the Gulf of Saint-Tropez, alongside Port Grimaud, Les Cigales and Le Gros Pin.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The lake-city of Port Grimaud is very close. It is also the starting point of the <a href="/fiche-balade-sentier-littoral-grimaud">coastal path to Saint-Tropez or Sainte-Maxime</a>.</p>
